        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: dépanner",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dépanner — \"to get going again (after a breakdown); to help out\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Un voisin nous a dépannés avec ses câbles. A neighbour got us going again with his cables."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Also: tu peux me dépanner de dix euros? — can you lend me ten euros?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dépanner is built from dé- 'un-' and panne 'breakdown' (EtymDB). Panne is usually traced to a sailing phrase, mettre en panne, for a ship held still with its sails turned against the wind."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
